<p>A <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2026/11/23/a-new-faster-emacs-package-to-do-image-triage/">couple days ago</a> I started typing a new Emacs mode for really fast image viewing and triage.  (Click the link to read the rationale behind all this.)  But after working with it for some hours, I realised that the layout just wasn&#8217;t ideal:</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25.jpeg"><img decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="543" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-126046" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25.jpeg 1384w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-300x194.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-1024x662.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-768x497.jpeg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>If we&#8217;re doing a line based layout, you have to have really small thumbnails &#8212; otherwise you get so little information density in a screenful.</p>
<p>And then I realised that the file names have very little interest.  The size is only interesting if it&#8217;s zero &#8212; that&#8217;s a broken image, but a missing thumbnail would also tell me that.  The date is the most interesting thing, but since I&#8217;m sorting by date, it&#8217;s not that vital anyway.</p>
<p>So:</p>
<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="538" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-126047" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-1.jpeg 1388w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-1-300x192.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-1-1024x657.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-25-1-768x492.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>A grid!  Well, duh.</p>
<p>The package can do both layouts, but defaults to using a grid now.</p>
<p>The image details are still available, both as tooltips, via a command, and shown automatically when walking through the images with <tt>TAB</tt>:</p>
<p><video autoplay loop muted poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/posterOVcZaP001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/video-hb5Ceh.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>I think that&#8217;s kinda usable?  Gives you an overview while tabbing through images.  Fast.  Vroom vroom.</p>
<p>You can find the code on <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/image-triage.el" data-cached-time="2026-11-25T13:01:20" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cache-2026-11-25-web.webp">Microsoft Github</a>.</p>
